Z||000000||Flash Box||11-14-2000||02:13 PM||||Yesterday I was talking to a guy that owns a '84 SE and it is fully loaded he told me that his car books for around $1100. I told him about my '84 Se and he said that the way it is now it would only bring a couple of hundred dollars and when I get it finished it would only bring around $500. Is he right?<BR>Here is some stuff about my '84:<BR>It has A/C, new tires new battery, new paint job (Although it needs buffed and wet sanded) Everything works (headligths, A/C, etc.<BR>Here is what it needs:<BR>Headliner and driver's seat need fixed<BR>Wheel stud needs replaced (which I have)<BR>Smog pump belt<BR>Rear hatch lock needs replaced<BR>The rear brake cailpler is starting to stick.<P>------------------<BR>"5 out of 4 people have a problem with fractions."||||reg||5
Z||000001||Old Lar||11-14-2000||02:47 PM||||The 1984 Fieros are the least desirerable Fiero except for the Pace car.  Unless it is in top shape, they are probably worth $1000 at best.  If you look at the Blue book values an 88 GT with T-tops in typical shape (100000K miles) is worth $3-5K.  Now if it has been well maintained and all the parts are present and working it may be worth more.  Any hobby car it value is determined by the market.  So if you want to have a valuable car, restore it and don't ever drive it.  Which is no real fun.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Cheever2||11-14-2000||06:51 PM||||Uh, yeah, he's right.  Er, ah, I'll give you $xxx to take it off your hands.<P>No, wait.  I couldn't do that to a PFF member.<P>It's worth what you decide it's worth.  Don't let anybody tell you different.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||wkayl||11-14-2000||07:19 PM||||Last Jan a guy ran a stop sign and hit my son' loaded 84 SE.  The guy's insurance paid us $1,700 for it.  It had 110,00 miles, new tires.  All options worked, but needed normal 84 Fiero stuff (drivers seat, paint, etc..).||||reg||1
